### Academic Analysis

The computational efficiency of range queries is defined by the trade-off between update and query costs. The naive strategy utilizes a contiguous array where point updates are performed in $\Theta(1)$ time, but range sum queries require iterating over $k$ elements, resulting in a worst-case time complexity of $O(N)$ and a tight bound of $\Theta(N)$ for queries. Consequently, for $Q$ operations, the total time complexity is $O(Q \cdot N)$. In contrast, the Segment Tree optimizes this by pre-calculating partial sums in a binary tree structure. Both point updates and range queries are performed in $\Theta(\log N)$ time, reducing the total complexity to $O(Q \log N)$. The space complexity for the naive approach is $\Theta(N)$, while the Segment Tree requires $\Theta(N)$ space, specifically allocating up to $4N$ nodes to accommodate the tree structure in a linear array.

The construction of the Segment Tree follows the recurrence relation $T(N) = 2T(N/2) + \Theta(1)$, which, by the Master Theorem, resolves to $\Theta(N)$. For traversal (both update and query), the recurrence is $T(N) = T(N/2) + \Theta(1)$, yielding $\Theta(\log N)$. The theoretical bound on the tree height is $O(\log N)$ because the Segment Tree is a balanced binary tree. Specifically, since the number of leaves is $N$, the height $h$ is the smallest integer such that $2^h \ge N$, which implies $h = \lceil \log_2 N \rceil$. This logarithmic height ensures that any path from the root to a leaf is bounded, preventing the linear degradation seen in the naive approach.

Despite the asymptotic superiority of the Segment Tree, physical hardware constraints introduce critical trade-offs. The naive array benefits from superior **spatial locality**; because elements are stored contiguously, the CPU can leverage cache lines to load blocks of data, making the $O(N)$ sum operation faster in practice than a pointer-heavy structure of the same complexity. The Segment Tree, particularly when implemented with objects or non-contiguous indices, suffers from increased cache misses as it jumps across memory addresses to traverse the tree. Furthermore, the Segment Tree imposes a memory overhead of approximately $4\times$ that of the naive array. Thus, for very small $N$, the constant factors and cache efficiency of the naive approach may outperform the Segment Tree, but as $N$ scales, the logarithmic growth of the Segment Tree inevitably dominates.
